    overview:
      "A comprehensive journey through North India's cultural heartland. From Delhi to Udaipur's lakes, Ranakpur and Kumbhalgarh forts, Deogarh's rural ramble, Pushkar's sacred lake, Jaipur's pink city, the Taj Mahal, Lucknow's nawabi heritage, Allahabad's sangam, and Varanasi's spiritual ghats.",
    images: ["/India/Jaipur.jpg", "/India/Agra.jpg", "/India/Udaipur.jpg"],
    details: `Day 01: ARRIVE DELHI

You will be met and assisted on arrival in Delhi and transferred to your hotel. The rest of the day is at leisure.

Day 02: DELHI

After breakfast proceed for the full day city tour of Old & New Delhi. In Old city visit Red Fort built by Emperor Shah Jahan; Jama Masjid, one of the largest mosques in India; Raj Ghat- the memorial of Mahatma Gandhi and enjoy a walking tour in Chandni Chowk. In the afternoon visit Qutab Minar, Humayun's Tomb, India Gate. Also drive past the President's house, Parliament house, Government secretariat buildings and Connaught place. Overnight is at the hotel.

Day 03: TO UDAIPUR

We begin our journey by flying to Udaipur, the city of lakes, arrive and check – in at hotel. In the evening you will enjoy a leisurely boat ride on LAKE PICHOLA. With its main attractions being the two palaces on the islands in the lake, Jagniwas and Jagmandir. The city palace extends along the eastern shore of the lake and you can go for a boat ride from Bansi Ghat.

Day 04: IN UDAIPUR

Udaipur is also a center of historic arts and crafts of Mewar. During a guided tour you will visit the CITY PALACE built in 1725, JAGDISH TEMPLE built in 1651, FATEH SAGAR and SAHELIYON KI BARI - another small ornamental garden, which used to be the garden-of-maids for the royal family.

Day 05: TO KUMBHALGARH

We continue our journey by road to Kumbhalgarh. On the way, we visit Ranakpur – one of the biggest and most important Jain temples in India. The main temple is the 'Chaumukha temple', or Four-Faced temple. Built in 1439, this huge, superbly crafted temple has 29 halls supported by 1444 pillars – of which no two are alike. We also visit Kumbhalgarh's impressive fort. The walls of the fort stretch some 36km and enclose many temples, palaces, gardens and water storage facilities.

Day 06: TO DEOGARH

Today we continue to Deogarh, which is only 70 kms and 1 hour away. Deogarh Mahal is an imposing structure built in the 17th century. It stands atop a hill and offers a commanding view of the Aravalli mountain range. Built in 1670 A.D. by Rawat Dwarka Dasji as a family residence, the gates that always remained closed to ward off enemies are now open to the guests.

Day 07: IN DEOGARH

You will enjoy a "Rural Ramble" today - a Jeep Safari, a drive into the countryside with an escort who knows the area and surrounding villages quite well. Visit the tribes and local villagers to see their way of living. Interact with them and share a cup of tea while you take pictures. This safari is a true insight into India's rural life. Return to your resort later in the day.

Day 08: TO PUSHKAR

From Deogarh, we continue our journey to the holiest and most ancient of Hindu pilgrim centres, Pushkar. The beautiful lake in Pushkar is surrounded by 52 bathing ghats (steps). Pushkar has the only Brahma (creator of the universe) temple in India. In the evening, we visit Pushkar lake and witness the arati (worship) and chanting ceremony at the Brahma temple.

Day 09: IN PUSHKAR

While in Pushkar, we also visit nearby Ajmer, one of the most important centers of pilgrimage for India's Muslims. The great Sufi saint Khwaja Mu'inuddin Chishti (1143-1235) is buried at the Dargah Sharif in Ajmer. We also visit the Arhai-din-ka Jhonpra, built in 1155, one of the finest monuments of medieval India.

Day 10: TO JAIPUR

We continue our journey by surface to the vibrant capital of Rajasthan, Jaipur. On the way, we stop at Kishangarh, a charming little town famous for having the finest school of miniature painting in eighteenth century India. Jaipur is popularly known as the 'pink city'.

Day 11: IN JAIPUR

This morning drive 11kms to visit Amber Fort, the ancient capital of the State until 1728. You can have the unique experience of traveling on elephant back to and from the top of the hill. Rest of the day is free to explore the colorful town independently. In the afternoon visit the Observatory, the Palace of Winds and the City Palace and Museum. Return to the hotel for overnight stay.

Day 12: TO AGRA

We continue our journey by surface to Agra. Firstly we stop en route at BHARATPUR BIRD SANCTUARY, which boasts of more than 350 different species of resident & migratory birds. Enjoy a bicycle ride or cycle-rickshaw ride here for an hour. Later drive to FATEHPUR SIKRI, founded in 1571 by Emperor Akbar as a thanksgiving to a Muslim saint, Sheikh Salim Chishti. This 16th century capital of Moghul emperor Akbar is a complex of forts, palaces and mosques built in sandstone.

Day 13: IN AGRA AND OVERNIGHT TRAIN TO LUCKNOW

After breakfast proceed for half day city tour of Agra. Visit the red sandstone Agra Fort and the famous white marble mausoleum, the Taj Mahal - the greatest monument to love and one of the wonders of the modern world. Rest of the day is at leisure. In the evening, transfer to the Tundla railway station for our train, Avadh Express to Lucknow (2315 / 0630hrs).

Day 14: ARRIVE IN LUCKNOW

Arrive in the wee hours of the day in Lucknow and drive to your hotel. The decadent Muslim rulers controlled a region of north-central India for about a century after the decline of the Mughal empire. Later visit the Bara Imambara or Great Imambara built in 1784 by Asaf-ud-Daulah. Also visit the imposing Rumi Darwaza and the Hussainabad Imambara (Chhota Imambara) built by Mohd. Ali Shah in 1837.

Day 15: TO ALLAHABAD

Today we journey for 5 hours (228 kms) to Allahabad. For the Hindus, Allahabad is particularly sacred because it is at the junction ('sangam') of the Ganga and the Yamuna. Here, we visit the confluence of the Ganga and Yamuna rivers, considered to have great soul-cleansing powers. We also visit the Anand Bhawan, the shrine to the Nehru family, and experience a boat-cruise at sunset.

Day 16: TO VARANASI

Our journey of 125 kms and less than two hours today takes us to Varanasi – one of the holiest places in India. Hindu pilgrims come to bathe in the waters of the Ganges. Visit Sarnath - where lord Buddha preached his first sermon. A major Buddhist centre, Sarnath lies 10 kilometres north east of Varanasi. Between the 3rd century BC and the 11th century AD, several Buddhist structures were built here. Most of Sarnath's monuments are set in large gardens.

Day 17: TO DELHI

Early in the morning, enjoy a boat ride on the River Ganges. Visit temples like Kashi Vishwanath temple, Bharat Mata Mandir, Durga temple and Alamgir mosque. After a relaxing morning in Varanasi we fly back to Delhi.

Day 18: DEPARTURE

Our tour finishes with breakfast, and then, you are transferred to the airport to board your international flight.`,
